    "name": "Related Party Loans (c) — purchase after default",
    "text": "(c) The Manager shall purchase (or cause one of its affiliates to purchase) from the Company, at par, any Related Party Loan in which any material event of default has occurred and is continuing for ninety (90) consecutive days."
  },
  {
    "id": "c047",
    "name": "Other Funds or Businesses; Allocations of Portfolio Loan Opportunities",
    "text": "Other Funds or Businesses; Allocations of Portfolio Loan Opportunities B-4 Partners LLC, an affiliate of the Manager owned and managed by Walter Ng and Dr. Bruce Horwitz, currently owns and manages R.E. Loans, LLC (‘R.E. Leans”), a licensed California finance lender with a business purpose similar to the Company’s. As of November 2007, R.E. Loans owns a loan portfolio of approximately 60 loans with aggregate principal value of $750 million. R.E. Loans is not anticipated to make additional portfolio loans, but it may take four to six years for its current portfolio to mature or be liquidated. Additionally, in the future, the Manager may also sponsor other funds formed to conduct business similar to that of the Company (together with R.E. Loans, the “Other Sponsored Investment Funds”). If Other Sponsored Investment Funds have capital to loan at the same time as the Company, there will then exist conflicts of interest on the part of the Manager as to whether to offer a particular loan opportunity to the Company or to these other funds. The Manager will decide which loans are appropriate for funding by the Company or by Other Sponsored Funds after consideration of all relevant factors, including the size of the loan, portfolio diversification, remaining term of the Company and the relative amount of uncomnnitted capital available for operations and lending."
  },
  {
    "id": "c048",
    "name": "Manager’s other ventures",
    "text": "Also, the Manager may engage for its own account, or for the account of others, in other business ventures, similar to that of the Company or otherwise, and neither the Company nor any Noteholder shall be entitled to any interest therein."
  },
  {
    "id": "c049",
    "name": "Different compensation for other investors",
    "text": "The compensation structure applicable to the Manager in connection with loans that are arranged or originated for investors other than the Company or Other Sponsored Investment Funds may be different than the compensation payable by the Company to the Manager for comparable services provided to the Company. Depending on the circumstances at a given point in time, devoting time or effort to provide such services to others than the Company may be more lucrative to the Manager than the compensation payable to the Manager in connection with the Company. As a result, there may exist a financial incentive for the Manager to arrange or originate loans for private investors outside the Company or for Other Sponsored Funds."
  },
  {
    "id": "c050",
    "name": "Reliance regarding Noteholder obligations",
    "text": "Accordingly, the Noteholders must rely on the Manager to act in good faith to cause the Company to meet its obligations to the Noteholders under the terms of the Notes and the Note Purchase Agreement."
  },
  {
    "id": "c051",
    "name": "Allocation of Management Time",
    "text": "Allocation of Management Time The Company will not have independent management and it will rely on the Manager for the operation of the Company. The Manager and its Principals will devote only so much time to the business of the Company as is reasonably required. The Manager and its Principals will have conflicts of interest in allocating management time, services and functions among the Company, existing business interests other than the Company and Other Sponsored Funds which it may organize or other business ventures in which it may be involved. The Manager believes it has sufficient staff available to be fully capable of discharging its responsibilities to all such entities."
  },
  {
    "id": "c052",
    "name": "Lack of Independent Legal Representation",
    "text": "Lack of Independent Legal Representation The Company has not been represented by independent legal counsel to date. The use by the Manager, Servicer and the Company of the same counsel in the preparation of this Memorandum and the organization of the Company may result in the lack of independent review. Prospective investors must rely on their own legal counsel for legal advice in connection with this investment."
  },
  {
    "id": "c053",
    "name": "Sale of Defaulted Loans or Real Estate Owned to Affiliates",
    "text": "Sale of Defaulted Loans or Real Estate Owned to Affiliates In the event a Portfolio Loan goes into default or the Company becomes the owner of any real property by reason of foreclosure on a Portfolio Loan, the Manager’s first priority will usually be to arrange the sale of the loan or property for a price that will permit the Company to recover the full amount of its invested capital plus accrued but unpaid interest and other charges, or so much thereof as can reasonably be obtained in light of current market conditions. In order to facilitate such a sale, the Manager may arrange a sale to persons or entities controlled by or affiliated with the Manager or the Servicer (e.g., to another entity formed by the Manager or its affiliates), for the express purpose of acquiring defaulted loans or foreclosure properties from lenders such as the Company. The Manager will be subject to conflicts of interest in arranging such sales since it will represent both parties to the transaction. For example, the Company and the potential buyer will have conflicting interests in determining the purchase price and other terms and conditions of sale. The Manager’s decision will not be subject to review by any outside parties."
  },
  {
    "id": "c054",
    "name": "Affiliate-sale pricing and financing standards",
    "text": "The Manager shail undertake to resolve these conflicts by setting a purchase price for each defaulted loan or property which is not less than any of the following: (i) the independently appraised value of such loan or property, if any, at the time of sale; (ii) the amount of any third party offer already received, if any; or (i1i) the total amount of the Company’s investment in the property. The Company’s investment is deemed to include without limitation the following: the unpaid principal amount of the loan upon which the Company foreclosed, all unpaid interest accrued to the date of foreclosure, expenditures made to protect the Company’s interest in the property such as payments to senior lienholders and for insurance and taxes, all costs of foreclosure (including attorneys fees actually incurred to prosecute the foreclosure or to obtain relief from stays in bankruptcy), and any advances made by or on behalf of the Company for any of the foregoing. A portion of the purchase price may be paid by the affiliate executing a promissory, note in favor of the Company, secured by a deed of trust on the property being sold. Such seller financing provided by the Company will not be deemed a Related Party Loan."
  },
  {
    "id": "c055",
    "name": "Transactions between the Company and Other Sponsored Funds, Private Investors",
    "text": "Transactions between the Company and Other Sponsored Funds, Private Investors The Company may purchase or sell, from time to time, loans for its portfolio from or to Other Sponsored Funds or private investors or lenders doing business with the Manager or its affiliates. Without limiting the foregoing, the Company expects to purchase mortgage loans from, or later into co-investment agreements with, R.E. Loans as may be determined by the Manager, on behalf of the Company, and B-4 Partners LLC, on behalf of R.E. Loans. In order to mitigate conflicts of interest that arise in connection with the Ioan purchase from, sale to, or co-investment with Sponsored Investment Funds or Private Clients, such transactions will be made in compliance with lending criteria for such loans established by the Note Purchase Agreement."
  },
  {
    "id": "c056",
    "name": "IV. Portfolio Loan Criteria and Policies — General Standards for Mortgage Loans",
    "text": "IV. Portfolio Loan Criteria and Policies General Standards for Mortgage Loans The Company will engage in the business of making loans secured by deeds of trust that encumber real estate located primarily in the United States, its territories and possessions. The Company may also invest indirectly in a loan by acquiring an ownership interest in an entity formed for the sole purpose of holding a qualifying loan. To the extent the Manager believes it to be in the best interests of the Company, the Company may also make or invest in construction loans for the purpose of constructing various types of residential and/or commercial properties, which construction loans would be secured by such properties. The Company may also make occasional loans to individuals and entities collateralized by loans that are secured by deeds of trust. The Company’s loans will not be insured or guaranteed by any governmental agency or private entity."
  },
  {
    "id": "c057",
    "name": "Property and loan-to-value guidelines",
    "text": "The Company expects to select loans for investment pursuant to a strict set of guidelines set forth below, which guidelines are designed to set standards for the quality of the real property security given for the loans. ‘Type of Property/Loan Loan to Value Ratio Improved Property (whether residential, commercial or industrial) 70% Land 50%"
  },
  {
    "id": "c058",
    "name": "Priority of Mortgages",
    "text": "Priority of Mortgages Most Company loans are expected to be secured by a first deed of trust on the real property (the “Security Property”) which is to be used as security for the loan. The Company’s investment in loans that are secured by a junior deed of trust will not exceed twenty percent (20%) of the Company’s entire Loan Portfolio."
  },
  {
    "id": "c059",
    "name": "Geographic Area of Lending Activity",
    "text": "Geographic Area of Lending Activity The Company will limit its lending activity primarily to deeds of trust on properties located in the United States, its territories and possessions (“U.S. Praperties”), but may invest up to 20% of total Company Capital in loans secured by non-U.S. Properties if the loan meets the Company’s lending criteria."
  },
  {
    "id": "c060",
    "name": "Loan-to-Value Ratios",
    "text": "Loan-to-Value Ratios The amount of the Company’s loan combined with the outstanding debt secured by any senior deed of trust on the security property generally will not exceed the percentages stated above, based a valuation of the security property as determined by either the Manager or an independent written appraisal. Appraisals and loan-to-value ratios may be determined on an as-completed basis with respect to construction or home improvement loans, however, loan funds may be withheld to be paid out upon inspection as improvements are made."
  },
  {
    "id": "c061",
    "name": "Loan-to-value exceptions",
    "text": "Finally, the foregoing loan-to-value ratio will not apply to purchase-money financing offered by the Company to sell any real estate owned (acquired through foreclosure) or to refinance an existing loan that is in default at the time of maturity. In such cases, the Manager shall be free to accept any reasonable financing terms that it deems to be in the best interests of the Company, in its sole discretion. No such ~12- CONFIDENTIAL of 48 Case3:14-cv-00993-SI Documenti7-1 Filed08/05/14 Pagei21 of 268 purchase money financing, if provided to any person or entity affiliated with the Company, the Manager, the Members, or the Servicer, or their respective members or principals, shall constitute a Related Party Loan,"
  },
  {
    "id": "c062",
    "name": "Terms of Loans",
    "text": "Terms of Loans The term of Company loans will vary at the discretion of the Manager. Company loans will generally have a term of between one (1) and six (6) years. Loans may be amortizing (i.e., the original principal amount of the loan will be partially or fully repaid in equal monthly installments of principal and interest over the term), or may provide for monthly payments of interest only during the term with a “balloon payment” due at the end of the term."
  },
  {
    "id": "c063",
    "name": "Credit Evaluations",
    "text": "Credit Evaluations The Manager may consider the income level and general creditworthiness of a borrower, and any guarantor, to determine a borrower’s ability to repay the Company loan according to its terms, but such considerations are subordinate to a determination that a borrower has sufficient equity in the security property to satisfy the loan-to-value ratios described above. Therefore, the Company may make loans to borrowers who are in default under other of their obligations (e.g., to consolidate their debts) or who do not have sources of income that would be sufficient to qualify for loans from other lenders such as banks or savings and loan associations."
  },
  {
    "id": "c064",
    "name": "Escrow Conditions — funding condition",
    "text": "Escrow Conditions Company loans will be funded through an escrow account handled by the Manager or a qualified title insurance or escrow company, The escrow agent will be instructed not to disburse any funds until the following conditions are met:"
  },
  {
    "id": "c065",
    "name": "Escrow Conditions (a) — title insurance",
    "text": "(a) Satisfactory title insurance coverage will be obtained for all ioans, with the title insurance policy naming the Company as the insured and providing title insurance in an amount equal to the principal amount of the loan. Title insurance insures only the validity and priority of the Company’s deed of trust, and does not insure the Company against loss by reason of other causes, such as diminution in the value of the security property, over-appraisals, borrower’s defaults, etc."
  },
  {
    "id": "c066",
    "name": "Escrow Conditions (b) — casualty insurance",
    "text": "(b) Satisfactory fire and casualty insurance has been obtained for all loans, or only casualty insurance in the event of a Loan secured by unimproved land, which insurance shall name the Company as loss payee in an amount equal to the principal amount of the Company’s loan. (See “VII. Risk Factors ~ Uninsured Losses,”)"
  },
  {
    "id": "c067",
    "name": "Escrow Conditions (c) — mortgage insurance",
    "text": "(c) The Manager does not intend to arrange for mortgage insurance, which would afford some protection against loss if the Company foreclosed on a loan and there were insufficient equity in the security property to repay all sums owed."
  },
  {
    "id": "c068",
    "name": "Escrow Conditions (d) — loan documents and ownership",
    "text": "(d) All loan documents (notes, deeds of trust, etc.) and insurance policies will name the Company as payee and beneficiary or additional loss insured, as applicable. In the event the Company purchases loans, the Company shall receive assignments of all beneficial interest in any documents related to each Loan so purchased. Company investments in Loans will not be held in the name of the Manager or any other nominee."
  },
  {
    "id": "c069",
    "name": "Loan Servicing",
    "text": "Loan Servicing It is anticipated that ail Company loans will be “serviced” (i.c., loan payments will be collected and other administrative services performed) by the Servicer, which is expected also to act as either the initial lender or loan broker in connection with the origination of most or all Company loans. The Servicer will be compensated for such loan servicing activities. (See “iI. Management - Compensation to Management.”)"
  },
  {
    "id": "c070",
    "name": "Portfolio Borrower payment procedure",
    "text": "Portfolio Borrowers will make loan payments monthly in arrears, i.¢., with respect to the preceding month, and will be instructed to mail their checks or money orders either directly to the Company or to the Servicer for deposit in the Servicer’s trust account and subsequent disbursement to the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c071",
    "name": "Loans to Related Parties — portfolio policy",
    "text": "Loans to Related Parties The Company may make or invest in Related Party Loans, which as noted, may raise significant conflicts of interest. (See “Ji, Management - Conflicts of Interest - Loans to Related Parties”). Aggregate principal amounts of Related Party Loans will not exceed 20% of total Company assets upon the closing of the Related Party Loan in question; making of a Related Party Loan must not be on terms more favorable to the borrower than similar loans negotiated at arm’s-length and must be purchased from the Company by the Manager or its affiliate rf the Loan is in default more than 90 days."
  },
  {
    "id": "c072",
    "name": "Purchase of Loans from Servicer",
    "text": "Purchase of Loans from Servicer The Company will buy or originate most or all of its mortgage loans from or through Servicer, which is in the business of originating and servicing mortgage loans. The Company may also purchase loans from unrelated third parties. Loans purchased by the Company shall not be in default at the time of purchase and must otherwise satisfy the foregoing lending guidelines. Generally, the purchase price to the Company for any such loan will not exceed the par value of the note or its fair market value, whichever is lower, but the Manager may purchase loans from unaffiliated parties for a premium if the Manager believes the total purchase price is fair and reasonable and in the best interests of the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c073",
    "name": "Transactions between the Company and Sponsored Investment Funds, Private Clients",
    "text": "Transactions between the Company and Sponsored Investment Funds, Private Clients The Company may purchase or sell loans from or to Sponsored Investment Funds, including R.E. Loans, or private Investors doing business with the Manager or its affiliates. The Company may also enter into co-lending transactions including inter-creditor and other agreements for such purpose or to achieve comparable with Sponsored Investment Funds, including R.E. Loans, or private investors or lenders doing business with the Manager or its affiliates. Such transactions will be in compliance with criteria established therefor in the Note Purchase Agreement. In summary, any loan proposed for the Company’s investment that is a purchase from, sale to, of a co-investment with, any such Sponsored Investment Fund or private investor, shali meet all of the relevant Company lending criteria for a non-affiliated loan, shall be priced at fair market value or better for the Company, and shall be on such terms as are customary for the geographical market that is the location of the real property collateral or the borrower, assuming for this purpose an arm’s length negotiation among equal parties, and, if not a new loan, shall be a fully performing loan at the time of its acquisition or co-investment. In the case of a co-investment with a Sponsored Investment Fund or private investor, such co-investment will be on terms no more favorable to such Sponsored Investment Fund or private investor than it is to the Company. Such transactions may involve significant conflicts of interest (See “Ji, Management - Conflicts of Interest - Transactions between the Company and Sponsored Investment Funds, Private Clients”.)"
  },
  {
    "id": "c074",
    "name": "Transactions with Noteholders",
    "text": "Transactions with Noteholders None of the Company, its Manager, principals or affiliates is precluded from (i) selling a property to any Noteholder in connection with a foreclosure, including with purchase financing, or (ii) from making a loan to, purchasing a loan from or entering into a loan or co-lending transaction or activity with any Noteholder, provided that any transaction with a Noteholder that is a Related Party Loan, or with a Noteholder who is also a Sponsored Investment Fund, private investor or lender doing business with the Manager, its Principals or affiliates, must meet the criteria established by the Note Purchase Agreement for such affiliated transactions."
  },
  {
    "id": "c075",
    "name": "Loan Diversification",
    "text": "Loan Diversification After the second year of the Company’s term, the Company will not enter into any new commitment to make a loan where the cumulative principal amount of tranches of such loan, as funded, exceeds 20% of the capital of the Company as of the date of such commitment,"
  },
  {
    "id": "c076",
    "name": "Reserve Fund",
    "text": "Reserve Fund A contingency reserve fund may be retained for the purpose of covering unexpected cash needs of the Company, if the Manager believes it to be in the best interests of the Company. The amount of this reserve fund, if any, would be established by the Manager, and may be funded by the Manager from any source, including capital raised from the sale of Notes. This reserve fund may be held in cash, bank accounts, certificates of deposit, money market accounts, short-term bankers’ acceptances, publicly traded bond funds or other liquid assets."
  },
  {
    "id": "c077",
    "name": "Sale of Loans Generally — nonperforming loans and foreclosed property",
    "text": "Sale of Loans Generally The Company will make mortgage loans for investment and does not expect to engage in real estate operations in the ordinary course of business {except as may be required if the Company forecloses on a property on which it has invested in a mortgage loan and takes over ownership and management of the property). The Company may also sell non-performing Portfolio Loans or foreclosed property securing Portfolio Loans, or sell an interest in such collateral, to the Servicer or to an affiliate of the Company, the Principals or the Servicer, for the purposes of restructuring the Portfolio Loan or re-positioning the property for sale."
  },
  {
    "id": "c078",
    "name": "Sale of Loans Generally — occasional sales",
    "text": "The Company does not presently intend to invest in mortgage loans primarily for the purpose of reselling such loans in the ordinary course of business; however, the Company may occasionally sell mortgage loans or enter into inter-creditor agreements when the Manager determines that it appears to be advantageous for the Company to do so, based upon then current interest rates, the length of time that the loan has been held by the Company, and the overall investment objectives of the Company."
  },
  {
    "id": "c079",
    "name": "Leveraging the Portfolio",
    "text": "Leveraging the Portfolio If the Manager deems it to be in the best interest of the Company, the Company may borrow funds from a third party lender (which may but is not required to be a bank or other financial institution) in order to fund additional mortgage loans. By leveraging the Company’s loan portfolio, the Company can increase the yield to the Company. This increased yield will result if the interest earned by the Company on its leveraged loans exceeds the interest that must be paid by the Company on the funds borrowed from a third party lender. This “spread” between the interest eared on a leveraged loan and the interest paid on the borrowed funds used to make the loan will accrue to the Company. However, leveraging a Portfolio Loan entails certain risks to the Company that would not otherwise be present if the Company funded all ~15- CONFIDENTIAL of 48 Case3:14-cv-00993-SI Documenti7-1 Filed08/05/14 Pagei24 of 268 of its Portfolio Loans from its own funds. For example, to the extent the Company makes Portfolio Loans at long-term fixed interest rates while it borrows funds at short-term variable interest rates, the Company will be subject to the risk that prevailing interest rates (and the Company’s associated borrowing costs) will rise above the levels earned by the Company on the fixed rate portion of its loan portfolio, causing losses to the Company. (See “Risks and Other Important Factors - Risks of Leverage.”)"
  },
