 {"clause_id": "c111", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Manager is said to bear 'the operating, legal and administrative expenses of the Company,' whereas Note Purchase Agreement §5.5(a) provides that the Company shall bear all costs, fees and expenses of its formation, operation and general administration, including legal and accounting fees. Who pays the Company's operating and legal costs is stated in opposite terms in the two documents.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.31}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c112", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This term caps Company Expenses at the greater of $500,000 or 1% of outstanding Principal, whereas Note Purchase Agreement §5.5(a) makes the Company bear all costs of formation, operation and administration with no cap at all. Whether the Company's expense burden is capped depends on which document governs.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26},
   {"issue": "The cap phrase 'not to exceed a maximum of $500,000 or 1% of aggregate Principal of outstanding Notes, whichever amount is greater' is placed after 'organizational fees and costs' but before the listed taxes, accounting, audit, communications and rent, so it is unclear whether the ceiling covers the whole list or only organizational costs. The syntax leaves the scope of the cap undetermined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c113", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The reimbursement obligation is unlimited, which cannot be squared with the dollar ceiling on Company Expenses in the immediately preceding term (c112). A cap on the Company's expenses and an uncapped duty to reimburse the Manager for those same expenses cannot both operate.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c114", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Loan Service Fee is payable by the Company 'to the Servicer,' whereas Note Purchase Agreement §5.4 provides that the Company shall pay the Loan Servicing Fee to the Manager (or the Manager's designee). Who is the Company's payee for servicing compensation differs between the documents.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
